NNAAMMEE
     ddvvmmrrppdd..ccoonnff — DVMRP routing daemon configuration file

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The dvmrpd(8) daemon implements the Distance Vector Multicast Routing
     Protocol as described in draft-ietf-idmr-dvmrp-v3-11 DVMRP Version 3.

     The ddvvmmrrppdd..ccoonnff config file is divided into the following main sections:

     _M_a_c_r_o_s
           Definitions of variables that can be used later, simplifying the
           configuration file.

     _G_l_o_b_a_l _c_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_a_t_i_o_n
           Global settings for dvmrpd(8).  A number of global settings can be
           overruled in specific areas or interfaces.

     _G_r_o_u_p_s
           Groups are used to group similar interfaces, simplifying configura‐
           tion.

   MMaaccrrooss
     A macro is defined with a command of the form _n_a_m_e=_v_a_l_u_e.  The macro _n_a_m_e
     can contain letters, digits, and underscores and cannot be a reserved
     word (for example, aarreeaa, iinntteerrffaaccee, or hheelllloo--iinntteerrvvaall).  Within unquoted
     arguments, the string $_n_a_m_e is later expanded to _v_a_l_u_e.

     For example:

           hi="60"
           group {
                   interface em0 {
                           query-interval $hi
                   }
           }

     The same can be accomplished by specifying the query-interval globally or
     within the group declaration.

   GGlloobbaall ccoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
     All interface related settings can be configured globally, per group and
     per interface.  The only settings that can be set globally and not over‐
     ruled are listed below.

     mmffcc--uuppddaattee (yyeess|nnoo)
             If set to yyeess, the kernel Multicast Forwarding Cache is updated
             with information from the daemon.  The default is yyeess.

   GGrroouuppss
     Groups can be used to easily configure similar interfaces.  All inter‐
     face-specific parameters can be configured per group, overruling the
     global settings.

     ggrroouupp   Specify a group section, grouping one or more interfaces.

                   group {
                           robustness 4

                           interface em0
                           interface em1
                   }

   IInntteerrffaacceess
     Each interface can have several parameters configured individually, oth‐
     erwise they are inherited.

           interface em0 {
           }

     Interface-specific parameters are listed below.

     iiggmmpp--vveerrssiioonn _n_u_m_b_e_r
             Do not use a newer version than specified.  Valid range 1–2.  The
             default version used is 2.

     llaasstt--mmeemmbbeerr--qquueerryy--ccoouunntt _c_o_u_n_t
             The default value is equal to the default robustness variable;
             valid range is 1–255.

     llaasstt--mmeemmbbeerr--qquueerryy--iinntteerrvvaall _s_e_c_o_n_d_s
             The default value is 10; valid range is 1–65535.

     mmeettrriicc _c_o_s_t
             Set the interface metric a.k.a. cost.  The default value is 1;
             valid range is 1–31.

     ppaassssiivvee
             Prevent transmission and reception of IGMP and DVMRP packets on
             this interface.

     qquueerryy--iinntteerrvvaall _s_e_c_o_n_d_s
             Set the query interval.  The default value is 125 seconds; valid
             range is 1–65535 seconds.

     qquueerryy--rreessppoonnssee--iinntteerrvvaall _s_e_c_o_n_d_s
             Set the query-response-interval; note that this must be less than
             the query-interval.  The default value is 100 seconds; valid
             range is 1–65535 seconds.

     rroobbuussttnneessss _f_a_c_t_o_r
             Set the robustness factor; note that this value might have an im‐
             pact on other variables.  The default value is 2; valid range is
             1–4.

     ssttaarrttuupp--qquueerryy--ccoouunntt _c_o_u_n_t
             Set the startup-query-interval, used during startup to speed up
             the process of building the IGMP table for an interface.  The de‐
             fault value is equal to the default robustness variable; valid
             range is the same as the robustness variable.

     ssttaarrttuupp--qquueerryy--iinntteerrvvaall _s_e_c_o_n_d_s
             Used during startup, see the startup-query-interval.  The default
             value is query-interval / 4; valid range is the same as the
             query-interval.

